    "reading": {
        "body": "Epic Systems embeds AI into clinical workflows and patient interfaces across its healthcare software platform, with tools for documentation, decision support, and patient engagement integrated into products serving hospitals and practices.\n\nThe company's AI deployment sits at a critical juncture: healthcare facilities depend on Epic's systems to manage patient data at scale, and adding AI to those systems means clinical decision support, documentation assistance, and patient-facing chat interfaces now operate on patient records in real time. This integration means Epic's computational load grows with AI adoption across its installed base. The regulatory surface area is substantial. Patient data exposure and HIPAA compliance are inherent to the architecture, not bolt-on concerns. FDA software-as-a-medical-device classification adds another layer of constraint on what changes the company can ship and how quickly.\n\nThe data covers Epic's AI products and regulatory considerations but does not address the compute infrastructure required to run these systems, whether Epic operates its own data centers or relies on cloud providers, or what power and cooling demands this workload creates. It also does not indicate whether these AI features are available to all customers or represent capacity-constrained rollouts. For understanding how healthcare AI scales within the broader constraint model, the infrastructure underlying these products remains opaque.",
